# Raw upstream feeds — regenerable via fetch.py, and large. Deliberately not
# committed: they churn three times a day, so git would store fresh blobs for no
# diffing benefit. Actions passes them between jobs as artifacts instead.
# data/ is the part that earns version control.
raw/

# Run logs
data/*.out

# The dead-letter queue. Regenerated in full by every normalize.py run, ~4 MB,
# and read by nothing -- not the site, not validate.py. Committing it three
# times a day was doubling the repo's growth to store a file no page has ever
# served. The counts that matter survive in meta.json (`stats`), which is
# committed, so the trend line stays in git history; only the 26,000 individual
# rejected titles are dropped. CI keeps them as a build artifact, which is the
# right retention for something you read when auditing the classifier and never
# otherwise. Regenerate locally any time with `python3 normalize.py`.
data/*/rejected.json

# This run's changed events, handed to the alert matcher. Not a durable
# artifact — event-history.jsonl is the ledger.
data/event-changes.json

# Development page cache — raw venue pages kept so parser changes can be
# re-applied without re-crawling. Local working files, never published.
data/page-cache/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# Astro / Vercel
node_modules/
dist/
.astro/
.vercel/
.env
.env.*.local

# Grove project metadata — local tooling config, not part of the build.
.grove/

# Build output
dist/
